// An lvalue's *value*, given its type and its address.
//
// A scalar or a pointer is loaded. A struct or an array is not: its value is
// its own address, which is C's array-to-pointer decay and, for a record, the
// only thing the flat-store model can mean by "the value of a struct" without
// copying it.
//
// The parentheses matter. A frame address emits as `fp + 24`, which is safe
// inside `peek32(...)` and safe as the left operand of anything, but wrong as
// the right operand of a subtraction: `q - p` on two decayed arrays would
// otherwise read `fp + 32 - fp + 8`, which is 32, not 24. Parenthesising here
// rather than in var_addr keeps every `peek32(fp + 24)` in the generated
// project spelled the way the reference lowering in tests/lowering/ spells it,
// and pays the extra brackets only where an address actually escapes as a
// value.
val_txt(int ty, string s) {
  string out = "(" + s + ")";
  if(k_of(ty) == "prim" || k_of(ty) == "ptr") {
    out = load_txt(ty, s);
  }
} return string out;
